<title>PHILIPPINES: Manila's PHIBOR rates continue to rise.</title>
<headline>Manila's PHIBOR rates continue to rise.</headline>
<dateline>MANILA 1997-04-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Philippine interbank offered rates (PHIBOR) edged higher on Friday in response to an outflow of foreign funds in recent days, traders said.</p>
<p>Banks had been getting their dollars from PHIBOR to meet the requirements of overseas funds which have decided not to renew their fixed income placements because of the low domestic interest rates, a dealer said.</p>
<p>PHIBOR represents the rates at which banks are willing to swap pesos for dollars.  </p>
<p>Average rates quoted by 20 banks were:</p>
<p>			  APRIL 4		 APRIL 3</p>
<p>    1 W		9  7/16		 9  3/8</p>
<p>    2 W		9  9/16		 9  3/8</p>
<p>    1 M		9  9/16		 9  5/16</p>
<p>    2 M		9  9/16		 9  7/16</p>
<p>    3 M		9  9/16		 9  7/16</p>
<p>    6 M		9  5/8		  9  9/16</p>
<p>    12M		9 13/16		 9  3/4</p>
<p>    VAL		7-Apr		   4-Apr</p>
<p>--LILIAN KARUNUNGAN (632) 841-8937</p>
